1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,525
Most engineers answer that question
by reading a vendor's blog post.

2
00:00:08,565 --> 00:00:11,753
The post is readable, well
illustrated, and selective.

3
00:00:11,793 --> 00:00:16,655
It tells you that something exists. It
doesn't tell you what your pipeline will do

4
00:00:16,695 --> 00:00:17,253
on Monday.

5
00:00:17,593 --> 00:00:21,214
This course ends today, and after
today nobody selects for you.

6
00:00:21,254 --> 00:00:25,687
The sources that do answer the question
are already on your disk: one file of

7
00:00:25,727 --> 00:00:30,393
release notes per version, one document
of planned breaking changes, and a manual

8
00:00:30,433 --> 00:00:33,705
page for every command at the
version you have installed.

9
00:00:33,745 --> 00:00:38,470
The last skill of the course is reading
them in a fixed order, in fifteen minutes.

10
00:00:38,510 --> 00:00:43,001
Hold on to the question on screen. Before
the demo you'll have five steps that

11
00:00:43,041 --> 00:00:43,582
answer it.

12
00:00:45,422 --> 00:00:50,030
Welcome back to Git and GitHub Deep
Mastery. Pull up a chair, one last time.

13
00:00:50,070 --> 00:00:54,861
This is the final video. It has two halves
and one purpose: that you can follow

14
00:00:54,901 --> 00:00:59,141
Git's development from primary sources,
without a tutorial in between.

15
00:00:59,481 --> 00:01:03,829
The first half is about sources. What
the build option about Rust tells you.

16
00:01:03,869 --> 00:01:08,448
Which tools around Git are worth knowing
by name. How to read release notes, the

17
00:01:08,488 --> 00:01:13,124
BreakingChanges document and the GitHub
Changelog, and what each is a source for.

18
00:01:13,464 --> 00:01:18,218
The second half is the workflow by which
Git itself is developed: commits sent as

19
00:01:18,258 --> 00:01:23,189
e-mail. A commit is one saved snapshot of
a project, with a message and the names of

20
00:01:23,229 --> 00:01:28,161
the people behind it. You create a patch
series with git format-patch, apply it with

21
00:01:28,201 --> 00:01:33,132
git am, and send a second version with a
range-diff, a comparison of two versions of

22
00:01:33,172 --> 00:01:34,316
a series of commits.

23
00:01:34,656 --> 00:01:39,225
In videos 20 and 21 you learned that a
commit has an author and a committer, and

24
00:01:39,265 --> 00:01:43,142
trailers. Today you see the workflow
those two identities exist for.

25
00:01:44,982 --> 00:01:46,560
Here's what you'll walk away with.

26
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:48,313
After this video you can:

27
00:01:48,653 --> 00:01:53,094
Say what the build option about Rust tells
you and what the BreakingChanges document

28
00:01:53,134 --> 00:01:53,574
promises.

29
00:01:53,914 --> 00:01:58,075
Read a release's notes in a fixed order
and decide what it changes for a team.

30
00:01:58,415 --> 00:02:01,762
Follow the GitHub Changelog
for dated platform changes.

31
00:02:02,102 --> 00:02:06,374
Create a patch series with a cover letter,
apply it with git am, and explain why

32
00:02:06,414 --> 00:02:08,152
author and committer then differ.

33
00:02:08,492 --> 00:02:12,452
Send a second version of a series and
show what changed with git range-diff.

34
00:02:14,292 --> 00:02:20,145
Rust in Git. Rust is a programming language,
and parts of Git are now written in it.

35
00:02:20,185 --> 00:02:25,758
BreakingChanges gives the milestones. Rust
code entered Git as optional in 2.49.

36
00:02:25,798 --> 00:02:30,038
It was auto-detected by one of
the two build systems in 2.52.

37
00:02:30,078 --> 00:02:35,861
It is enabled by default in both from 2.55.
And it becomes mandatory in 3.0, unless

38
00:02:35,901 --> 00:02:41,614
the impact on distributions leads to a
deferral. The long-term-support promise you

39
00:02:41,654 --> 00:02:46,174
heard in video 199 exists for
platforms without a Rust toolchain.

40
00:02:46,514 --> 00:02:51,238
For you as a user nothing changes
except where the binary comes from.

41
00:02:51,278 --> 00:02:55,311
In video 199 the first transcript
printed "rust: disabled".

42
00:02:55,351 --> 00:03:01,110
The reason is the distributor: Homebrew
builds its Git 2.55.0 with NO_RUST=1, as the

43
00:03:01,150 --> 00:03:06,357
textbook read in the formula on 2 October
2026. So "enabled by default" is a

44
00:03:06,397 --> 00:03:10,707
statement about the build system,
and each distributor decides.

45
00:03:10,747 --> 00:03:16,368
That is what the build option tells you:
what your binary was built with, not what

46
00:03:16,408 --> 00:03:18,094
the project's default is.

47
00:03:18,434 --> 00:03:23,864
Stacked workflows. A stack is a chain of
small dependent branches, each reviewed on

48
00:03:23,904 --> 00:03:29,400
its own. In Git the tools are git rebase
--update-refs, git range-diff for comparing

49
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:34,870
versions, and now git history, whose default
of moving all descendant branches fits

50
00:03:34,910 --> 00:03:35,397
a stack.

51
00:03:35,737 --> 00:03:41,531
GitHub, not Git. Stacked pull requests
entered public preview on GitHub on 30 July

52
00:03:41,571 --> 00:03:45,302
2026, with a gh stack extension
for the command line.

53
00:03:45,342 --> 00:03:50,283
That is described from the changelog
and not exercised in this course.

54
00:03:50,623 --> 00:03:55,498
Tools worth knowing by name. None is
part of Git, and none is needed for this

55
00:03:55,538 --> 00:03:55,945
course.

56
00:03:56,285 --> 00:03:59,762
The last row is the one that
matters for the 3.0 defaults.

57
00:03:59,802 --> 00:04:04,673
BreakingChanges names exactly these three
libraries as the ones that must support

58
00:04:04,713 --> 00:04:09,282
reftable before it becomes the default.
A tool built on a library reads your

59
00:04:09,322 --> 00:04:14,254
repository with that library's abilities,
not with those of the git you installed.

60
00:04:14,594 --> 00:04:18,990
Release notes. Every release has one
file, on GitHub and on your disk.

61
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:23,363
Each file has the same three parts.
First "UI, Workflows & Features".

62
00:04:23,403 --> 00:04:27,800
Then "Performance, Internal
Implementation, Development Support etc.".

63
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:30,588
And last "Fixes since"
the previous version.

64
00:04:30,928 --> 00:04:35,657
Read the first part completely: it is short
and it is where behavior changes are.

65
00:04:35,697 --> 00:04:40,484
Search the rest for the commands you depend
on. The style is terse, one bullet per

66
00:04:40,524 --> 00:04:45,253
topic, often without the option names. The
procedure that works: find the bullet,

67
00:04:45,293 --> 00:04:50,022
open the manual page of that command at
the new version, and try it in a sandbox.

68
00:04:50,362 --> 00:04:54,857
One caution from the textbook: a
notes file is not proof of a release.

69
00:04:54,897 --> 00:04:55,894
The tag list is.

70
00:04:56,234 --> 00:05:00,881
BreakingChanges. Its headings are the whole
structure: the procedure, the changes,

71
00:05:00,921 --> 00:05:05,567
the removals, and what will not be deprecated.
Each item links to the mailing-list

72
00:05:05,607 --> 00:05:07,167
thread where it was decided.

73
00:05:07,507 --> 00:05:12,403
The other channels. The maintainer's "What's
cooking" messages list every topic in

74
00:05:12,443 --> 00:05:16,436
flight and its state, and Git Rev
News summarizes the list monthly.

75
00:05:16,476 --> 00:05:21,010
Vendor posts, the highlight posts of
GitHub and GitLab for each release, are

76
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:25,825
readable and selective: use them to learn
that something exists, and the primary

77
00:05:25,865 --> 00:05:27,450
text to learn what it does.

78
00:05:27,790 --> 00:05:32,316
Quick quiz. GitHub changes how one
of its platform features behaves.

79
00:05:32,356 --> 00:05:37,889
Where is that written first: A, in Git's
release notes, B, in the GitHub Changelog,

80
00:05:37,929 --> 00:05:40,440
or C, in BreakingChanges? Your answer?

81
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:50,533
GitHub, not Git: the Changelog. The answer
is B. The GitHub Changelog is the primary

82
00:05:50,573 --> 00:05:53,746
source for platform behavior,
dated entry by entry.

83
00:05:53,786 --> 00:05:58,975
A feature there has a state, preview or
generally available, and often a plan gate.

84
00:05:59,015 --> 00:06:04,141
Nothing in it describes the git program on
your machine, and a Git release changes

85
00:06:04,181 --> 00:06:07,039
nothing on github.com
until GitHub deploys it.

86
00:06:07,379 --> 00:06:12,199
A routine of fifteen minutes per release.
Here's the answer to the question from the

87
00:06:12,239 --> 00:06:16,827
opening. Five steps, and this order is
the answer to today's interview question.

88
00:06:16,867 --> 00:06:20,819
Read the first section of the notes.
Search for your team's commands.

89
00:06:20,859 --> 00:06:25,331
Look at the diff of BreakingChanges.
Decide whether any default you rely on is

90
00:06:25,371 --> 00:06:29,555
named. Write down the minimum version
for anything new you want to adopt.

91
00:06:29,895 --> 00:06:34,255
The patch-based workflow, in one sentence.
Git itself is developed by sending

92
00:06:34,295 --> 00:06:38,771
commits as e-mail: git format-patch turns
commits into message files, reviewers

93
00:06:38,811 --> 00:06:43,057
answer on the list, and the maintainer
applies accepted series with git am.

94
00:06:43,097 --> 00:06:44,657
No pull request is involved.

95
00:06:44,997 --> 00:06:48,184
Precisely. A contribution
starts on a topic branch.

96
00:06:48,224 --> 00:06:53,499
Each commit carries a Signed-off-by trailer,
from git commit -s, by which the author

97
00:06:53,539 --> 00:06:58,435
certifies the right to submit it. A
trailer is a labelled line at the end of a

98
00:06:58,475 --> 00:06:59,384
commit message.

99
00:06:59,724 --> 00:07:04,541
git format-patch writes one file per commit
plus an optional cover letter, and git

100
00:07:04,581 --> 00:07:07,621
send-email posts them to
the project's mailing list.

101
00:07:07,661 --> 00:07:12,359
GitGitGadget is a bridge that turns a pull
request on GitHub into such a series.

102
00:07:12,699 --> 00:07:17,754
After review the author sends a complete
new version, v2, v3, with a range-diff

103
00:07:17,794 --> 00:07:22,977
against the previous one. The maintainer
queues topics on the branch seen, merges

104
00:07:23,017 --> 00:07:28,265
them to next for testing and then to master.
And maint receives fixes for the last

105
00:07:28,305 --> 00:07:28,781
release.

106
00:07:29,121 --> 00:07:34,043
A patch file is a commit in mail form.
The From line with an ID and a fixed date

107
00:07:34,083 --> 00:07:39,129
marks the format. The From:, Date: and
Subject: headers become author, author date

108
00:07:39,169 --> 00:07:44,153
and subject. The message body follows,
then three dashes, then a diffstat that is

109
00:07:44,193 --> 00:07:46,510
ignored on application, then the diff.

110
00:07:46,850 --> 00:07:50,992
Why author and committer differ after
git am. The author comes from the mail

111
00:07:51,032 --> 00:07:53,852
headers. The committer is
the person who ran git am.

112
00:07:53,892 --> 00:07:56,878
The two identities of a commit
exist for this workflow.

113
00:07:57,218 --> 00:08:02,200
Where you will meet it. In three places
besides the Git project: the Linux kernel

114
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:06,725
and other list-based projects, vendors
who send fixes as patch files, and

115
00:08:06,765 --> 00:08:10,445
environments where two repositories
cannot reach each other.

116
00:08:10,485 --> 00:08:15,653
git am also underlies git rebase --apply.
On GitHub the same ideas have other names:

117
00:08:15,693 --> 00:08:20,674
the pull request is the cover letter, a
force-pushed branch is a new version, and

118
00:08:20,714 --> 00:08:22,720
"compare" is a weaker range-diff.

119
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:28,543
Two cautions from section 14D.13. git
am refuses to start when the index has

120
00:08:28,583 --> 00:08:34,283
changes. And git apply without --index
or --3way patches working files only and

121
00:08:34,323 --> 00:08:39,806
creates no commit: it is a tool for
trying a patch, not for integrating one.

122
00:08:41,646 --> 00:08:46,289
Two pictures help. For the sources, think
of three kinds of document about a law.

123
00:08:46,329 --> 00:08:50,509
The statute itself: the manual page
at your version, and BreakingChanges.

124
00:08:50,549 --> 00:08:55,192
The official gazette, which says what
changed in each edition: the release notes,

125
00:08:55,232 --> 00:08:59,932
and for the platform the GitHub Changelog.
And the newspaper article about it: the

126
00:08:59,972 --> 00:09:03,921
vendor post. You read the newspaper
to learn that there is a new law.

127
00:09:03,961 --> 00:09:06,465
You don't plead in court
from the newspaper.

128
00:09:06,805 --> 00:09:10,398
Where the picture breaks: a statute
applies when it is published.

129
00:09:10,438 --> 00:09:15,037
A Git release applies to you when the binary
on your machine, on your runner and in

130
00:09:15,077 --> 00:09:18,614
your IDE's library changes, and
those are three different dates.

131
00:09:18,954 --> 00:09:22,360
For the patch workflow, think
of a letter with an enclosure.

132
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:25,922
The envelope says who wrote it
and when, and what it is about.

133
00:09:25,962 --> 00:09:28,966
The letter explains why. The
enclosure is the change.

134
00:09:29,006 --> 00:09:33,504
The recipient who files it signs the
register: that signature is the committer.

135
00:09:33,544 --> 00:09:38,099
Nothing in the envelope says where in the
recipient's cabinet the paper belongs,

136
00:09:38,139 --> 00:09:42,867
which is why a patch gets a new commit ID
wherever it is applied, and why the cover

137
00:09:42,907 --> 00:09:44,935
letter can carry a base-commit line.

138
00:09:45,275 --> 00:09:50,578
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds. Write
three headers of a patch file, From:,

139
00:09:50,618 --> 00:09:55,262
Date: and Subject:, and beside each,
the part of the commit it becomes.

140
00:09:55,302 --> 00:09:59,088
Then say out loud what a commit
has that the file doesn't.

141
00:10:05,428 --> 00:10:09,048
Check your paper against the drawing.
The three headers become the author, the

142
00:10:09,088 --> 00:10:12,615
author date and the subject. Build the
rest top to bottom, with an arrow for

143
00:10:12,655 --> 00:10:16,462
everything that becomes part of the commit,
and end with the three things that are

144
00:10:16,502 --> 00:10:19,559
not in the file: the committer,
the committer date and the parent.

145
00:10:19,599 --> 00:10:22,000
Those three are why the
applied commit has a new ID.

146
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:24,939
Where the notes are.

147
00:10:25,279 --> 00:10:30,545
Into the lab. git --html-path prints
the documentation directory of your

148
00:10:30,585 --> 00:10:36,515
installation. 541 files of release notes.
And look at the list: a file for 2.54.1

149
00:10:36,555 --> 00:10:42,337
exists, and the course's research found
no tag and no tarball for that version.

150
00:10:42,377 --> 00:10:45,211
A notes file is not proof of a release.

151
00:10:45,551 --> 00:10:46,436
The three parts.

152
00:10:46,776 --> 00:10:51,819
Line 4, line 82, line 236. The
first part is under eighty lines.

153
00:10:51,859 --> 00:10:54,678
That's the part you read completely.

154
00:10:55,018 --> 00:10:59,946
Search for your commands. Predict: what
will the notes for 2.55 say about git

155
00:10:59,986 --> 00:11:01,559
history? Say it out loud.

156
00:11:06,399 --> 00:11:10,274
One bullet: "git history" learned
"fixup" command. That is all.

157
00:11:10,314 --> 00:11:15,308
No options, no example. From there you go
to the manual page and to a sandbox, as

158
00:11:15,348 --> 00:11:20,342
you did in video 200. Note the third hit:
the wording about Rust in the installed

159
00:11:20,382 --> 00:11:23,697
notes is vaguer than the
milestone in BreakingChanges.

160
00:11:23,737 --> 00:11:27,488
Which is why the course cites
the installed copy when it can.

161
00:11:27,828 --> 00:11:28,810
BreakingChanges.

162
00:11:29,150 --> 00:11:34,659
The headings: Procedure, Git 3.0, Changes,
Removals, and the superseded features

163
00:11:34,699 --> 00:11:40,209
that will not be deprecated. Line 83 is
the sentence from video 199: there is no

164
00:11:40,249 --> 00:11:45,898
planned release date. And the last command
prints one line per planned change of a

165
00:11:45,938 --> 00:11:50,129
default: five lines, the five
rows of the table in video 199.

166
00:11:50,169 --> 00:11:55,471
You have now verified a whole video
against a file on your own disk, in three

167
00:11:55,511 --> 00:11:56,096
commands.

168
00:11:56,436 --> 00:12:00,862
Create the series. 🟢 SAFE: git
format-patch writes files.

169
00:12:01,202 --> 00:12:05,816
Two commits, three files: a cover letter
numbered zero, and one file per commit.

170
00:12:06,156 --> 00:12:08,684
Read a patch file. This is the diagram.

171
00:12:09,024 --> 00:12:15,246
Find each part: the From line with 0cddac1,
the author, the date, the subject with

172
00:12:15,286 --> 00:12:21,202
its [PATCH 1/2] prefix, the trailer, the
three dashes, the diffstat, the diff.

173
00:12:21,542 --> 00:12:26,483
The cover letter is a template to fill in:
a subject and a blurb in capitals between

174
00:12:26,523 --> 00:12:30,397
asterisks. Below it, the list of
commits and the overall diffstat.

175
00:12:30,437 --> 00:12:35,259
And base-commit: the commit the series
applies to, which tells reviewers and tools

176
00:12:35,299 --> 00:12:36,208
where to put it.

177
00:12:36,548 --> 00:12:41,050
Apply it. The maintainer, in a repository
that has never seen the branch.

178
00:12:41,090 --> 00:12:44,784
🟡 CAUTION: git am creates new
commits on the current branch.

179
00:12:44,824 --> 00:12:49,326
Preview: git apply --check. Recovery:
git am --abort while it is stopped.

180
00:12:49,366 --> 00:12:54,366
Afterwards, the reflog of the branch.
Predict the author and the committer of the

181
00:12:54,406 --> 00:12:56,420
applied commits. Say it out loud.

182
00:13:01,260 --> 00:13:05,460
Author: Asha Rao. Committer: Ravi
Menon, the person who ran git am.

183
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:10,587
The sign-off trailer travelled in the
message. This is what the two identities of

184
00:13:10,627 --> 00:13:11,663
video 20 are for.

185
00:13:12,003 --> 00:13:13,626
Same change, new ID.

186
00:13:13,966 --> 00:13:17,139
Equal trees, equal patch
IDs, different commit IDs.

187
00:13:17,179 --> 00:13:21,739
A patch ID is computed from the change
alone. The content arrived intact.

188
00:13:21,779 --> 00:13:25,331
The committer, the committer
date and the parent are new.

189
00:13:25,671 --> 00:13:29,193
Version 2. Review asked for
casefold() instead of lower().

190
00:13:29,233 --> 00:13:32,141
This is the contributor's
branch as it was sent.

191
00:13:32,481 --> 00:13:37,438
The contributor keeps version 1 under
another name, casefold-v1, and rewrites the

192
00:13:37,478 --> 00:13:40,955
branch: two new commits, and
casefold now points at them.

193
00:13:41,295 --> 00:13:46,007
On its own, git range-diff judged the
rewritten first patch a total rewrite and

194
00:13:46,047 --> 00:13:50,639
printed it as removed and added. Its
creation factor defaults to 60, and in a

195
00:13:50,679 --> 00:13:55,632
two-line patch one changed line is a large
share. The second patch is marked equal.

196
00:13:55,972 --> 00:14:00,824
The files carry v2 in their names. And
the cover letter now contains a section

197
00:14:00,864 --> 00:14:05,716
"Range-diff against v1". format-patch
--range-diff uses a much higher creation

198
00:14:05,756 --> 00:14:10,859
factor, because it compares iterations of
one topic, so the same pair appears with

199
00:14:10,899 --> 00:14:15,563
an exclamation mark and a diff of the
two diffs: lower() became casefold().

200
00:14:15,603 --> 00:14:20,392
This is how a reviewer of version 2 sees
what changed since version 1 without

201
00:14:20,432 --> 00:14:21,960
reading everything again.

202
00:14:27,100 --> 00:14:32,465
Read before you apply: the first lines of
the patch, and git apply --stat for what

203
00:14:32,505 --> 00:14:34,706
the files would touch. Both are 🟢.

204
00:14:35,046 --> 00:14:40,485
git apply --check exits 0: the patch would
apply. Then git am, and the log shows the

205
00:14:40,525 --> 00:14:45,312
pattern again: author from the patch,
committer the person who applied it.

206
00:14:45,652 --> 00:14:50,986
The two trees are equal, and the two commit
IDs are not: 25c29be here, 92eba7f in

207
00:14:51,026 --> 00:14:56,294
the fork. Same content, different commit.
You could have predicted every line of

208
00:14:56,334 --> 00:14:58,218
this output from the diagram.

209
00:15:00,058 --> 00:15:01,706
Five mistakes to watch for.

210
00:15:02,046 --> 00:15:07,127
Deciding from a vendor post. Root cause:
highlight posts are selective, so they show

211
00:15:07,167 --> 00:15:11,821
that something exists and not what it
changes for your commands and defaults.

212
00:15:12,161 --> 00:15:15,367
Reading "enabled by default"
as "enabled in my binary".

213
00:15:15,407 --> 00:15:20,147
Root cause: the default belongs to the
build system, and each distributor decides

214
00:15:20,187 --> 00:15:20,973
how it builds.

215
00:15:21,313 --> 00:15:25,214
Looking for Git behavior in the
GitHub Changelog, or the reverse.

216
00:15:25,254 --> 00:15:30,065
Root cause: the Changelog is the primary
source for the platform only, and a Git

217
00:15:30,105 --> 00:15:33,824
release changes nothing on
github.com until GitHub deploys it.

218
00:15:34,164 --> 00:15:38,761
Expecting an applied patch to keep its
commit ID. Root cause: committer, committer

219
00:15:38,801 --> 00:15:43,285
date and parent are not in the patch file,
so the applied commit is a new object

220
00:15:43,325 --> 00:15:44,586
with the same patch ID.

221
00:15:44,926 --> 00:15:51,073
Integrating with git apply. Root cause:
without --index or --3way it patches working

222
00:15:51,113 --> 00:15:56,229
files only and creates no commit,
and it carries no author or message.

223
00:15:58,069 --> 00:16:02,913
Now, out of the lab. A platform team upgrades
Git on its build images twice a year.

224
00:16:02,953 --> 00:16:07,678
The engineer who owns the upgrade has a
routine, and it takes her a quarter of an

225
00:16:07,718 --> 00:16:08,679
hour per release.

226
00:16:09,019 --> 00:16:13,701
She opens the notes file of each new version
on her disk and reads the first section

227
00:16:13,741 --> 00:16:18,310
completely. She searches all three sections
for the commands in the team's release

228
00:16:18,350 --> 00:16:22,920
scripts. She compares the BreakingChanges
document with the copy from the previous

229
00:16:22,960 --> 00:16:23,370
upgrade.

230
00:16:23,710 --> 00:16:27,227
She checks whether any default
the team relies on is named.

231
00:16:27,267 --> 00:16:32,291
And she writes down, in the upgrade ticket,
the minimum version for anything new the

232
00:16:32,331 --> 00:16:37,235
team wants to use. For platform changes
she reads the GitHub Changelog separately,

233
00:16:37,275 --> 00:16:40,973
by date, and notes for each entry
its state and its plan gate.

234
00:16:41,313 --> 00:16:46,326
The same week a vendor sends a fix for
a tokenizer library as two patch files,

235
00:16:46,366 --> 00:16:49,825
because the vendor cannot
reach the team's repository.

236
00:16:49,865 --> 00:16:55,073
She reads the files, runs git apply --check,
applies them with git am on a review

237
00:16:55,113 --> 00:16:57,730
branch, and opens a pull request from it.

238
00:16:58,070 --> 00:17:02,479
The commits show the vendor's engineer
as author and her as committer, and the

239
00:17:02,519 --> 00:17:06,359
team's history says truthfully who
wrote the fix and who applied it.

240
00:17:06,399 --> 00:17:10,809
When the vendor sends a second version,
she asks for it with a range-diff, and

241
00:17:10,849 --> 00:17:14,232
reviews the difference between
the two versions in a minute.

242
00:17:16,072 --> 00:17:21,850
Your turn. Do Lab 42.3, "A
format-patch and am round trip", in

243
00:17:21,890 --> 00:17:24,383
lab-manual/m42-frontier.md.

244
00:17:24,723 --> 00:17:29,187
Before you apply the patches, predict
for the applied commits: the author, the

245
00:17:29,227 --> 00:17:33,806
committer, whether the tree will equal
the original's, and whether the commit ID

246
00:17:33,846 --> 00:17:38,541
will. Before the lab's failure scenario,
predict what git am will do when the base

247
00:17:38,581 --> 00:17:43,391
has moved, what state it will leave behind,
and which of its exits you know from the

248
00:17:43,431 --> 00:17:46,278
way video 182 thinks about
operations in progress.

249
00:17:46,618 --> 00:17:51,192
The challenge: read the release notes of
the Git version after the one installed,

250
00:17:51,232 --> 00:17:55,920
and write, for your team, one paragraph:
what changes, whom it affects, what to do.

251
00:17:57,760 --> 00:17:59,813
Read the question, then answer out loud.

252
00:18:04,653 --> 00:18:08,569
This is the last question of the
course, and it asks for a procedure.

253
00:18:08,609 --> 00:18:13,156
A strong answer names the documents by
what each is a source for, primary before

254
00:18:13,196 --> 00:18:16,482
secondary, and gives an order
with a reason for the order.

255
00:18:16,522 --> 00:18:21,069
It says where the documents are, so that
the answer doesn't depend on a website.

256
00:18:21,409 --> 00:18:26,243
It includes the step that turns reading
into knowledge: the manual page at the new

257
00:18:26,283 --> 00:18:31,236
version, and a sandbox. It separates the
git program from the platform and names the

258
00:18:31,276 --> 00:18:36,111
platform's own primary source. And it ends
on a limit: which of your tools doesn't

259
00:18:36,151 --> 00:18:40,153
use the git program at all, and so
won't change when you upgrade it.

260
00:18:41,993 --> 00:18:43,994
Let's land this, for the last time.

261
00:18:44,334 --> 00:18:49,791
The Rust line of git version --build-options
describes your binary; the project's

262
00:18:49,831 --> 00:18:52,981
milestones and promises
are in BreakingChanges.

263
00:18:53,321 --> 00:18:57,937
Release notes have three parts; read the
first completely, search the rest for your

264
00:18:57,977 --> 00:19:00,798
commands, then go to the
manual page and a sandbox.

265
00:19:01,138 --> 00:19:05,741
BreakingChanges is the source for planned
changes and removals; the GitHub Changelog

266
00:19:05,781 --> 00:19:10,052
is the source for dated platform changes;
vendor posts tell you that something

267
00:19:10,092 --> 00:19:10,438
exists.

268
00:19:10,778 --> 00:19:15,007
A patch file is a commit in mail form: git
format-patch writes it, git am applies

269
00:19:15,047 --> 00:19:18,960
it, the author comes from the file and
the committer is whoever applied it.

270
00:19:19,300 --> 00:19:23,807
A new version of a series is sent whole,
with a range-diff that shows what changed

271
00:19:23,847 --> 00:19:25,082
since the previous one.

272
00:19:26,922 --> 00:19:33,210
Read sections 14D.9 to 14D.14 and do the
Practice section, 14D.16, with its two

273
00:19:33,250 --> 00:19:39,859
drills. Read reference/references.md: it
is the list of primary sources behind this

274
00:19:39,899 --> 00:19:43,624
course, and from today
it is your reading list.

275
00:19:43,964 --> 00:19:48,588
That's the end of the course. You began
with a repository read file by file.

276
00:19:48,628 --> 00:19:53,621
You can now investigate a Git or GitHub
problem from first principles, explain the

277
00:19:53,661 --> 00:19:58,347
root cause with its layer, choose the
lowest-risk fix, verify it, prevent its

278
00:19:58,387 --> 00:20:02,520
recurrence, and find out for yourself
what the next release changes.

279
00:20:02,860 --> 00:20:05,970
So what now? Keep the course
materials working for you.

280
00:20:06,010 --> 00:20:10,781
The references file is your reading list.
The glossary is there whenever a colleague

281
00:20:10,821 --> 00:20:14,160
or an error message uses a word
you want to be exact about.

282
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:18,169
Your playbook and your weak-area
tracker are yours to keep up to date.

283
00:20:18,209 --> 00:20:22,866
The labs and the incidents can be generated
afresh whenever you want the practice.

284
00:20:22,906 --> 00:20:27,390
And when a new Git release comes out,
take your fifteen minutes with its notes.

285
00:20:27,730 --> 00:20:30,985
Thank you for working through
all of this with such care.

286
00:20:31,025 --> 00:20:35,494
It has been a pleasure to teach you. Look
at the state first, type second, and

287
00:20:35,534 --> 00:20:36,419
goodbye for now.
